{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Make for yourself two silver trumpets.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): these trumpets were of silver and were hammered (מקשה) from a single piece, and they served two purposes: one, to assemble the people or the princes, and the second, for the journeying — as it is written: ״והיו לך למקרא העדה ולמסע את המחנות״. When they blew in both of them it was a sign to assemble the people, and when they blew in a single trumpet alone it was a sign to assemble the princes, as it is written: ״ואם באחת יתקעו ונועדו אליך הנשיאים״ — for they are the designated ones of the people, therefore they would blow in one and it was a sign for them. Both this and that were with a teki'ah, which is a plain (פשוטה) blast, and פשוטה alludes to the attribute of mercy, for His right hand is stretched out (פשוטה) to receive penitents. But for the journeying of the camps they would blow a teru'ah, as it is stated: ״תרועה יתקעו למסעיהם״ — because the journeying was a sign of war, for the camps of Israel were setting out against the seven nations to do battle with them and to conquer the land. Therefore they would sound the teru'ah at the start of their journeying, for the teru'ah alludes to the attribute of judgment (דין), and it is what prevails in war. And because of this the verse mentions concerning the journeys (Numbers 9:23) ״על פי ה׳ ביד משה״, and this is what is written (Numbers 10:9): ״וכי תבאו מלחמה בארצכם והרעותם״, and Moses mentioned: ״וינוסו משנאיך מפניך״ — that is, from the attribute of judgment. And so the order of the matter was in the journeys: they would blow for each and every direction, for the four directions of the world, for the four camps of the four standards were stationed at the four directions of the world. And this is what is written: ״ותקעתם תרועה ונסעו המחנות החונים קדמה״, and ״ותקעתם תרועה שנית ונסעו המחנות החונים תימנה״ — and here you have the camps of the east and the camps of the south. ״תרועה יתקעו למסעיהם״ — for the journeys of the remaining ones, meaning that they would blow a third teru'ah for the journeying of the camps of the west and a fourth teru'ah for the journeying of the camps of the north, for each standard had its own journeying separately, as the verse proceeds to explain: ״והולך״ — ונסע דגל פלוני, and so all of them." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: ״עשה לך שתי חצוצרות כסף״ — this is what the verse says (Psalms 24:9): ״שאו שערים ראשיכם״ — that the gates were low, and he said: ״והנשאו פתחי עולם״ etc. The gates said to him: ״מי זה מלך הכבוד?״ Solomon, peace be upon him, answered: ״ה׳ עזוז וגבור״. He returned and said: ״ושאו פתחי עולם״ etc. They said to him: ״מי הוא זה מלך הכבוד?״ He answered and said: ״ה׳ צבאות הוא מלך הכבוד סלה״. Had he not told them ״ה׳ צבאות הוא מלך הכבוד״, the gates would have sought to fall upon him and crush his skull; but once he answered them thus, the gates immediately opened. And once they gave honor to the Holy One, blessed be He, the Holy One, blessed be He, said to them: By your lives — when I destroy My house, no creature shall have dominion over you. You may know this, for all the vessels of the Temple were exiled to Babylon, as it is stated (Daniel 1:2): ״ויתן ה׳ בידו את יהויקים מלך יהודה ומקצת כלי בית האלהים״ — but the gates of the Temple were hidden away in their place, as it is stated (Lamentations 2:9): ״טבעו בארץ שעריה״. And the Holy One, blessed be He, is called King of Glory because He shares of His glory with those who fear Him. How so? He is called Elohim, and concerning Moses it is written (Exodus 7:1): ״ראה נתתיך אלהים לפרעה״. He revives the dead, and concerning Elijah it is written (I Kings 17:23): ״ראי חי בנך״. He is called King, as it is written (Psalms 47:6): ״עלה אלהים בתרועה״ etc. And concerning Moses it is written (Deuteronomy 33:5): ״ויהי בישרון מלך״. Just as with a king, when he goes out they blow before him — so you too: ״עשה לך שתי חצוצרות כסף״ — so that when you bring Israel in, they will blow in them and they will assemble, as it is stated: ״ותקעו בהן ונועדו אליך כל העדה אל פתח אהל מועד״.<br><b>Make for yourself</b> — for yourself you make them, and no other may use them. You may know that even Joshua his disciple did not use trumpets but shofarot when he came to do battle against Jericho, which was the beginning of the conquest of the land of Israel — what is written there (Joshua 6:20)? ״וירע העם ויתקעו בשופרות״. And not only that, but even Moses himself, in his lifetime, they were hidden away, for so he says at the time of his passing (Deuteronomy 31:28): ״הקהילו אלי את כל זקני שבטיכם ושוטריכם״ — and where were the trumpets, that he says to them 'assemble' and does not say 'blow in the trumpets and they will gather'? Rather, they were hidden away, to fulfill what is said (Ecclesiastes 8:8): ״אין שלטון ביום המות״. And concerning King David it is written (II Chronicles 29:26): ״ויעמדו הלוים בכלי דוד״ etc. ״והשיר משורר וחצוצרות מחצצרים״ — but David himself used the lyre (כנור), as it is stated (Psalms 57:9): ״עורה כבודי עורה הנבל וכנור אעירה שחר״. A lyre was suspended above David's bed; when midnight arrived, a north wind would blow through it and it would play of itself. Immediately King David, peace be upon him, would arise, and all the disciples who were engaged in Torah study and would banish sleep from their eyes, until he would rise: ״עורה כבודי עורה הנבל וכנור אעירה שחר״. In His kingship, the Holy One, blessed be He, commanded this for the honor of Moses, for such is the protocol of a king: they blow the shofar before him, for thus he commands to gather his hosts. And because his attainment ascended to prophesy in the Great Name, the attribute of mercy, therefore He commanded them to make them of silver, and they were not fit to be made from other kinds of metals — as they said in tractate Menaḥot: The trumpets were to come from the work of silver; they made them from silver ingots (גרוטאות), valid; from other kinds of metals, invalid. And because the speech of mercy was singled out for him, face to face, a vision within a vision (מראה בתוך מראה), therefore they were two and were hammered (מקשה). And this is the secret of the verse ״ותקעו בהן ונועדו אליך כל העדה״: the blowing in both of them, which was a sign to assemble all Israel, alludes to the speech of mercy from within the fire to all Israel — and this is what is written (Deuteronomy 4:15): ״ביום דבר ה׳ אליכם בחרב מתוך האש״. And the blowing in one, which was a sign for the princes designated in the people, alludes to Moses, who was designated above all of them, who would hear from within the teki'ah — that is, the voice of the distinctive Name (השם המיוחד) — and he alone attains it and contemplates it. And the teru'ah for the journeying of the camps setting out to war alludes to the attainment of Israel, which is the teru'ah. And since no prophet ascended to this degree in prophecy to hear from the distinctive Name except him alone, therefore He commanded him that none should use the trumpets except him alone and no other — as it is written: ״עשה לך״, and our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: 'for yourself you make them, and no other uses them.' And He further added and said: ״והיו לך״ — meaning, these attributes are yours alone and not for another besides you. And because of the great holiness of the trumpets, and their being an instrument in which the attainments were alluded to, they required hiding away (גניזה), and they were hidden away in Moses' lifetime.<br><b>And do not</b> wonder that both of them were of silver, for since they were hammered (מקשה), it was impossible to make them except of silver — like the matter of the cherubim, which were of gold and hammered (מקשה), for mercy prevails and is intermixed with judgment. And so too here, with the trumpets, the attribute of judgment is weakened (רפה) and intermixed with mercy. And for this reason Joshua his disciple did not use the trumpets, for his attainment did not reach that level. However, he did use the shofar, to make known through it that the attribute of judgment is drawn against the enemies, for that is his attribute. And it is also the attribute of David, who used the lyre (כנור). And it is the techelet dyed from the blood of the ḥillazon, which is the fish found in the Sea of Kinneret — therefore this attribute is called כנור דוד (David's lyre), for it would play of itself. You find learned that each and every one used the instrument suited to him according to his attainment, his prophecy, and the attribute from which he draws — and this is clear."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:31:27.673623723Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}